  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-27719",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ {x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-4)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16-9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"$x=\\pm 4.$\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
